# latex closes a raw region on a STRING, where every family before it closed on
# a byte, and this file is built so a byte cannot pass it.
#
# `find_verbatim(lexer, "\\end{verbatim}")` walks forward keeping the end marked
# behind it and only stops on the whole keyword. The body here is `\end{x} $y`,
# which contains `\end` and `{`, so a close that matched the first byte `\`
# would stop at 17, a close that matched `\end` would stop at 17, and only a
# close that compares all fourteen bytes reaches 28. The `$y` is in for the same
# reason from the other side: latex's `$` is math mode outside a verbatim body,
# so a run that was NOT raw would build a math node in the middle of it.
#
# tree-sitter frames the body as one `comment` covering [16, 28) - the newline
# before it and the newline after are inside the run - and the two `\begin`/`\end`
# heads as `curly_group_text`. Claims are its tree.
roots 1
mends 0
holds verbatim_environment
holds comment
lacks ERROR
spans verbatim_environment 0 42
spans comment 16 28
